Rem Capability: net
Rem ==================================================================
Rem net.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: TCP/IP client and server
Rem
Rem A friendly layer over the raw TCP* builtins. Handles are Numbers,
Rem like file handles. Payloads are Strings carrying one byte per
Rem character (the same binary-safe convention as ReadFile).
Rem
Rem On a PLAIN socket the builtins never block: TCPRecv returns "" when
Rem nothing has arrived yet, TCPAccept returns 0 when no client is
Rem waiting. The Wait* helpers below turn that into blocking-style
Rem convenience by polling around Sleep, so a simple program reads as if
Rem it blocked while a game loop or server can still poll by hand.
Rem Everything here is effectful, like all i/o words — keep it at the
Rem edges.
Rem
Rem ---- a secured socket is the exception ---------------------------
Rem
Rem Secure(sock, host) upgrades a connected socket to TLS in place, and
Rem from that moment the contract for THAT HANDLE changes:
Rem
Rem     Recv     blocks until data, the peer closes, or 30s pass, and
Rem              returns "" only at end of input
Rem     Eof      reads a flag rather than polling
Rem     Ready    IS REFUSED — it aborts the program
Rem
Rem That is deliberate, not an oversight. Once TLS records wrap the
Rem stream a TCP-level poll stops telling the truth about plaintext: a
Rem partial record makes bytes look ready that decrypt to nothing yet,
Rem and a fully buffered record makes plaintext look absent when a read
Rem would return it at once. Rather than answer dishonestly, Ready
Rem refuses.
Rem
Rem The two worlds genuinely differ and both keep their own truth.
Rem Nobody polls a TLS socket in a game loop: TLS is here for
Rem request/response at the edges, while the polling contract is what
Rem scribbler windows and game loops on the loopback are built on. Since
Rem a program cannot ask a handle whether it is secured, know which kind
Rem you are holding — in practice you always do, because you secured it.
Rem
Rem Server-side TLS is not supported: Accept always hands back a plain
Rem socket. This is a TLS client.
Rem
Rem The whole family is gated: run the mill with --allow-net, or the
Rem first TCP word raises "network is disabled".
Rem
Rem     Def Main()
Rem         Let reply = Request("example.com", 80,
Rem             "GET / HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: example.com\r\n\r\n")
Rem         Print(reply)
Rem
Rem For HTTP over TLS, and for taking a reply apart, use https.shoddy —
Rem it is built on these words.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "str.shoddy"

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused; they are written here because net.shoddy is
Rem where a reader goes looking for them. The flat reference is section
Rem 10 of docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.
Rem
Rem HANDLES ARE NUMBERS, drawn from the same table of sixteen that
Rem binary files use. Payloads are Strings carrying ONE BYTE PER
Rem CHARACTER — the Latin-1 convention, the same one ReadFile uses.
Rem
Rem THE WHOLE FAMILY IS GATED behind --allow-net, with two exceptions
Rem that exist so it can be reached from guarded code at all:
Rem NetAllowed, which has to stay askable when the answer is no, and
Rem TryTcpConnect, which reports rather than dies. Everything else
Rem aborts if the network is off.
Rem
Rem ON A PLAIN SOCKET NOTHING BLOCKS. A secured one is the deliberate
Rem exception, and the header above says what changes.

Rem NetAllowed() As Boolean                           ( -- flag )
Rem     Whether this mill was started with --allow-net. NOT itself
Rem     gated, and that is the point: without it nothing could tell "no
Rem     network" from "no answer" without ending the run. A program
Rem     cannot turn the capability on; it can only ask.

Rem TCPConnect(host As String, port As Number) As Number
Rem                                                   ( host port -- h )
Rem     Connect and answer the handle. Bounded 10-second handshake;
Rem     aborts if unreachable, if the network is off, or if all sixteen
Rem     handles are taken.

Rem TryTcpConnect(host As String, port As Number) As Result
Rem                                                   ( host port -- result )
Rem     TCPConnect with all three of those deaths reported instead:
Rem     Ok(handle), or Err(why, 0) where why is CANNOT REACH
Rem     'host:port' (…) ending in one of NETWORK IS DISABLED, TOO MANY
Rem     OPEN SOCKETS, TIMED OUT or NO SUCH HOST OR REFUSED — a closed
Rem     set of Shoddy's own, never the platform's socket text.

Rem TryTcpRequest(host As String, port As Number, secure As Boolean, msg As String) As Result
Rem                                                   ( host port secure msg -- result )
Rem     A WHOLE CONVERSATION in one word: connect, optionally secure,
Rem     send, read to the end of the reply, close — answering Ok(reply)
Rem     or the same shape of Err. It exists because guarding the STEPS
Rem     is not enough: Send and Recv die eight ways between them on
Rem     ordinary network faults, so a caller composing guarded parts
Rem     would have to close the socket on each of a dozen error paths
Rem     and would leak it on the one it missed. Here the socket cannot
Rem     outlive the word. It is also the only network read that CANNOT
Rem     HANG — it is bounded by a deadline, where a poll-until-closed
Rem     loop never returns against a keep-alive peer.

Rem TCPListen(host As String, port As Number) As Number
Rem                                                   ( host port -- h )
Rem     Bind and listen, answering the listening handle. The host is an
Rem     IP literal: "127.0.0.1" for loopback, "0.0.0.0" for any.

Rem TCPAccept(h As Number) As Number                  ( h -- h2 )
Rem     A connection handle, or 0 when none is waiting. Never blocks,
Rem     which is what WaitAccept below polls around.

Rem TCPSend(h As Number, s As String)                 ( h s -- )
Rem     Send the bytes.

Rem TCPRecv(h As Number, max As Number) As String     ( h max -- s )
Rem     Up to max bytes; "" when nothing is pending OR the peer has
Rem     closed. TCPEof is what tells those two apart, and the pair is
Rem     why a drain loop needs both.

Rem TCPEof(h As Number) As Boolean                    ( h -- flag )
Rem     Whether the peer has closed. On a secured handle this reads a
Rem     flag rather than polling.

Rem TCPPoll(h As Number) As Boolean                   ( h -- flag )
Rem     Whether Recv or Accept would find something right now. REFUSED
Rem     on a secured handle: once TLS records wrap the stream a
Rem     TCP-level poll cannot answer honestly about plaintext, so it
Rem     aborts rather than lie.

Rem TCPPeer(h As Number) As String                    ( h -- s )
Rem     The remote end as "ip:port".

Rem TCPClose(h As Number)                             ( h -- )
Rem     Close the handle and free its slot.

Rem TCPSecure(h As Number, host As String)            ( h host -- )
Rem     Upgrade a connected socket to TLS IN PLACE. The host drives both
Rem     SNI and certificate name validation, and a failed handshake
Rem     closes the handle. From this moment that handle follows the
Rem     secured contract above — Recv blocks, Poll refuses. A program
Rem     cannot ask a handle whether it is secured, so know which kind
Rem     you are holding; in practice you always do, because you secured
Rem     it. Server-side TLS is not supported.

Rem ---- this machine's own words --------------------------------------

Rem ---- client ------------------------------------------------------

Def Connect(host As String, port As Number) As Number
    TCPConnect(host, port)

Def Send(sock As Number, s As String)
    TCPSend(sock, s)

Def SendLine(sock As Number, s As String)
    TCPSend(sock, s & "\r\n")

Rem Raw non-blocking read: up to n bytes, "" if none have arrived.
Def Recv(sock As Number, n As Number) As String
    TCPRecv(sock, n)

Rem True once the peer has closed its end.
Def Eof(sock As Number) As Boolean
    TCPEof(sock)

Rem True when a Recv (or Accept, on a listener) would find something now.
Def Ready(sock As Number) As Boolean
    TCPPoll(sock)

Def Peer(sock As Number) As String
    TCPPeer(sock)

Def Close(sock As Number)
    TCPClose(sock)

Rem Poll (yielding 5ms between tries) until at least one chunk is
Rem readable, then return it. Returns "" once the peer has closed.
Def WaitRecv(sock As Number) As String
    If TCPPoll(sock) Then
        TCPRecv(sock, 4096)
    Else
        Sleep(5)
        WaitRecv(sock)

Rem Poll until a chunk is readable or the deadline passes, whichever
Rem comes first; "" on expiry. WaitRecv waits forever, which is right for
Rem a peer that will close and wrong for one that will not — a keep-alive
Rem server that never hangs up spins WaitRecv until the heat death. Reach
Rem for this one when you cannot promise the peer closes.
Def WaitRecvFor(sock As Number, ms As Number) As String
    WaitRecvUntil(sock, Ticks() + ms)

Def WaitRecvUntil(sock As Number, deadline As Number) As String
    If TCPPoll(sock) Then
        TCPRecv(sock, 4096)
    Else
        If Ticks() >= deadline Then
            ""
        Else
            Sleep(5)
            WaitRecvUntil(sock, deadline)

Rem Read the whole response, chunk by chunk, until the peer CLOSES —
Rem HTTP/1.0 with Connection: close, which is what Request and HttpGet
Rem send. Against a peer that keeps the connection open this never
Rem returns; use WaitRecvFor and assemble the pieces yourself.
Rem
Rem Fragments into a list, joined once at the end. Building the answer
Rem with chunk & RecvAll(...) instead would copy the whole accumulated
Rem body per chunk — quadratic in the reply, which is exactly the shape a
Rem reader is — and would not be tail-recursive either, so a large body
Rem would grow the stack a frame per chunk as well.
Def RecvAll(sock As Number) As String
    Join(Reverse(RecvAllR(sock, { })), "")

Def RecvAllR(sock As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    Let chunk = WaitRecv(sock)
    If chunk = "" Then
        acc
    Else
        RecvAllR(sock, Prepend(chunk, acc))

Rem ---- TLS ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem Read the header: securing a handle changes what Recv, Eof and Ready
Rem mean on it, and Ready stops being answerable at all.

Rem Upgrade a connected socket to TLS in place. host drives both SNI and
Rem certificate name validation, so it must be the name the certificate
Rem was issued for — an IP address will not match one. A failed handshake
Rem takes the handle with it.
Def Secure(sock As Number, host As String)
    TCPSecure(sock, host)

Rem Connect and secure with the SAME host, which is the pairing that
Rem keeps SNI honest.
Def ConnectTls(host As String, port As Number) As Number
    Let s = Connect(host, port)
    Secure(s, host)
    s

Rem Drain a SECURED socket to end of input. Recv blocks there, so this
Rem needs no poll and no Sleep — and must not use WaitRecv or RecvAll,
Rem whose TCPPoll would abort on a secured handle.
Def RecvAllTls(sock As Number) As String
    Join(Reverse(RecvAllTlsR(sock, { })), "")

Def RecvAllTlsR(sock As Number, acc As List Of String) As List Of String
    Let chunk = Recv(sock, 4096)
    If chunk = "" Then
        acc
    Else
        RecvAllTlsR(sock, Prepend(chunk, acc))

Rem The secured mirror of Request: connect, secure, send, drain, close.
Def RequestTls(host As String, port As Number, msg As String) As String
    Let s = ConnectTls(host, port)
    Send(s, msg)
    Let reply = RecvAllTls(s)
    Close(s)
    reply

Rem Connect, send one message, read the full reply, close. The common
Rem request/response shape, with the handle managed for you.
Def Request(host As String, port As Number, msg As String) As String
    Let s = Connect(host, port)
    Send(s, msg)
    Let reply = RecvAll(s)
    Close(s)
    reply

Rem A minimal HTTP/1.0 GET, to prove the stack end to end. It sends only
Rem a Host header and speaks plaintext on port 80, which is enough for a
Rem demo and not enough for the modern web — most hosts now redirect to
Rem HTTPS, and many refuse a request with no User-Agent. See https.shoddy
Rem for the one that holds a real conversation.
Def HttpGet(host As String, path As String) As String
    Request(host, 80,
        "GET " & path & " HTTP/1.0\r\nHost: " & host & "\r\n\r\n")

Rem ---- server ------------------------------------------------------

Rem Loopback only — the safe default. Reachable from this machine alone.
Def Listen(port As Number) As Number
    TCPListen("127.0.0.1", port)

Rem Bind an explicit address to go wider, e.g. ListenOn("0.0.0.0", 8080).
Def ListenOn(host As String, port As Number) As Number
    TCPListen(host, port)

Rem Non-blocking accept: a connection handle, or 0 if none is waiting.
Def Accept(server As Number) As Number
    TCPAccept(server)

Rem Poll (yielding 5ms between tries) until a client connects.
Def WaitAccept(server As Number) As Number
    Let c = TCPAccept(server)
    If c = 0 Then
        Sleep(5)
        WaitAccept(server)
    Else
        c

Rem Accept one client, hand the connection to handler, then close it.
Def Serve1(server As Number, handler As [ Number -- ])
    Let conn = WaitAccept(server)
    Call(handler, conn)
    Close(conn)
